{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story, a[0][\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(a[0][\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story,a in c.items()]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story, a[0][\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(a[0][\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story,a in c.items()]' \"","aggregated_output":"\n### anthropic-ipo-stake gnews:09857e6bb52dbeb1ab300224 \n Nvidia in talks to invest in Anthropic’s mega IPO, sources say\nAnthropic is seeking to raise as much as US$100 billion (S$126.7 billion) that could value the artificial intelligence start-up at around US$2 trillion.\nAnthropic is planning a mega IPO to raise up to US$100 billion, potentially valuing the AI start-up at about US$2 trillion, with Nvidia considering a US$10 billion investment.\nNvidia's involvement as an anchor investor would boost confidence and deepen ties between the chipmaker and Anthropic, which relies heavily on Nvidia GPUs for computing power.\nAnthropic's valuation and revenue have surged rapidly, backed by major tech firms like Amazon and Google, with plans to complete the IPO before the US midterm elections in November.\nSAN FRANCISCO – Anthropic is in talks to bring Nvidia as an anchor investor into what could be the largest IPO in history, two people familiar with the matter told Reuters.\nAnthropic is seeking to raise as much as US$100 billion (S$126.7 billion) that could value the artificial intelligence start-up at around US$2 trillion, the people said.\nNvidia is considering investing up to US$10 billion in the IPO, one of the sources added.\nThe plans remain under discussion and could change, they cautioned, requesting anonymity because the discussions are confidential.\nNvidia’s potential involvement in the IPO, which has not previously been reported, would give Anthropic an early strategic backer for the outsized offering, while further deepening ties between the chipmaker and one of its major customers.\nBringing a deep-pocketed anchor investor like Nvidia on board could boost investor confidence in the listing, which is shaping up to be a major test of public-market appetite for the enormous valuations and capital requirements of frontier AI labs.\nAnthropic declined to comment. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\nAnchor investors are typically institutional investors that commit to buy a set portion of an IPO before it is marketed more broadly, providing an early vote of confidence in the shares.\nIt is increasingly common for mega IPOs to line up early institutional commitments, given the large amounts they seek to raise. Chip designer Arm’s anchor investors included Nvidia and Amazon, while Saudi Arabia’s PIF was among SpaceX’s anchor investors.\nThe deepened relationship highlights the complex ties between AI model developers and the companies supplying the enormous amounts of computing power they require. Anthropic relies heavily on Nvidia GPUs, while also seeking to diversify its chip suppliers as demand for Claude has strained its available computing capacity.\nIn November 2025, Nvidia said it would invest up to US$10 billion in the AI start-up as part of a broader partnership under which Anthropic committed to buy US$30 billion of Microsoft Azure computing capacity powered by Nvidia chips.\nAnthropic already counts the biggest tech companies in the world as backers. These include Amazon and Google, who are also its major compute suppliers.\nIn April, Anthropic said it would commit more than US$100 billion over a decade to Amazon’s AWS, while using more than one million of Amazon’s Trainium2 chips. It has also agreed with Google and Broadcom to add multiple gigawatts of TPU capacity.\nThe company has been trying to access more compute as its revenue has surged. It is also working to gain greater control over its hardware costs by forming an in-house team to design custom chips tailored to Claude.\nThe listing, which is expected to complete before the US midterm elections in November, would mark another jump in Anthropic’s valuation.\nThe Claude maker raised US$65 billion in May at a post-money valuation of US$965 billion. Its annualised revenue run rate had climbed above US$65 billion by the end of July, from about US$9 billion at the end of 2025, according to the company.\nThe potential valuation also hinges in part on company projections of roughly US$190 billion to US$200 billion in revenue in 2028, Reuters previously reported.\nThe expected Anthropic IPO added to a string of blockbuster listings that has propelled the US IPO market towards a record year, following Elon Musk’s SpaceX’s debut in June.\nUS IPOs, excluding special-purpose acquisition companies, have raised a record US$137 billion through the end of August, according to Dealogic. REUTERS\n\n### reflection-ai-deal gnews:0097a6928e13586221ccd7ab \n Nvidia is exploring ways to either increase its stake in Reflection AI or furnish the startup with more compute, per a person with knowledge of the talks.\nReflection's first open-weight model, Beam, launched earlier this month and targets coding and AI agent workloads for businesses and developers.\nNvidia's recent spree includes a $13 billion deal announced in September to buy Hugging Face and an estimated $20 billion licensing arrangement with Groq.\nNvidia is weighing a deeper partnership with Reflection AI, according to someone familiar with the conversations. Possibilities on the table include putting more money into the startup or supplying additional computing resources.\nThose discussions are still in the early innings and nothing has been finalized, the person said, requesting anonymity because the matter is private. Nvidia did not return a request for comment right away, while Reflection said it had no comment.\nThe Financial Times previously reported the talks and said they could even progress to a takeover bid. Structures under consideration include an acqui-hire that brings staff aboard while licensing the tech, a chip supply agreement, or a larger Nvidia equity position.\nEarlier this month, Reflection introduced its first open-weight model, Beam. In open-weight setups, developers release the model's weights so others can use and adapt the system freely.\nOn Monday, Reflection said Beam was built to perform well for enterprise and developer tasks such as coding and AI agents, while undercutting many rivals on cost. It also said Beam's advanced reasoning scores are comparable to GLM-5.2 from China's Z.AI, which is considered a leading open model.\nThe company was started by two former Google DeepMind researchers. The Wall Street Journal has reported that Reflection has discussed raising $2.5 billion at a $25 billion valuation.\nReflection has also signed billion-dollar agreements with Elon Musk's SpaceX and with cloud provider Nebius Group NV to lock in computing power for building its models.\nChipmaker partnerships decide which AI labs get the hardware they need. Market Briefs covers that supply chain free every weekday .\nNvidia's bigger bet on open-weight models\nA tie-up with Reflection would slot neatly into Nvidia's recent deal streak. The chipmaker, now the most valuable company in the world, has been using its balance sheet to nurture the wider AI ecosystem.\nRecent moves include a $13 billion agreement unveiled in September to purchase AI model company Hugging Face , and an approximately $20 billion licensing deal with chip startup Groq. Chief Executive Jensen Huang has been outspoken in favor of open-weight systems.\nThe push is partly about ensuring more US-led open-weight options. The idea is that stronger domestic offerings could limit startups' need to rely on Chinese open-weight models, which are far cheaper to run than US models from outfits like OpenAI and Anthropic PBC and not dramatically behind in smarts. Washington is also leaning in.\nIf Nvidia tightens its relationship with Reflection, it extends a clear pattern: bankroll the models, provide the chips, and shape where developers build. For everyday investors, the takeaway is simple enough: open-weight tools like Beam aim to cut costs for businesses, while Nvidia positions itself at the center of the picks-and-shovels economy that powers them.\nWhere Nvidia invests shapes who can compete at the frontier. Join Market Briefs free and follow the money .\nBy Andre Savage\nBy Nate Gregory\nBy Jackson Moreland\nBy Milena Thomas\nBy Kalbir Talwar\nBy Briefs Finance\n\n### hugging-face-acquisition gnews:143da8b9ceacebfb7b016e66 \n / Key facts: NVIDIA (NVDA) buys Hugging Face $12.93B; $500B, 500k GPUs\nKey facts: NVIDIA (NVDA) buys Hugging Face $12.93B; $500B, 500k GPUs\nNVIDIA (NVDA) agreed to buy Hugging Face for $12.93B on Sept 3, 2026. NVIDIA says Hugging Face stays open to broader AI community and will be integrated into NVIDIA’s software stack.\nNVIDIA says OpenAI’s Astra (GPT‑6) trained on 100,000+ Grace Blackwell systems; another 400,000 GPUs planned. Signals massive NVDA hardware demand for large‑scale AI training.\nNVIDIA says it’s teaming with major banks to mobilize over $500 billion in third‑party capital to finance AI infrastructure, targeting external funding for AI‑related infrastructure investments.\nNVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang says company compute infrastructure is a durable, revenue-driving asset and AI GPU demand remains strong; H100 rental rates reportedly rose about 22% to ~$3.28/hour.\nNVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AGI has arrived after OpenAI released GPT-6 Astra; critic Gary Marcus disputed the claim, arguing Astra doesn’t meet standard AGI criteria.\nNVIDIA (NVDA) is eyeing data-center builds in South Korea, citing the country’s reliable power—an important factor for AI infrastructure and big computing needs.\n\n### firmus-investment gnews:e68d5b0219b8755050bcdaf1 \n Nvidia-backed Firmus secured a multiyear agreement to provide OpenAI with computing capacity from two Malaysian data centers. Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator powerhouse supplying Firmus's next-generation infrastructure, fell approximately 1.5% to $226.86 in Tuesday morning trading.\nThe OpenAI deal pushes Firmus's committed customer capacity beyond 900 megawatts. The company currently operates two facilities and is building five more across the Asia-Pacific region, with future sites set to deploy Nvidia's Vera Rubin processors at scale. Firmus did not reveal the contract's financial terms, leaving investors without a clear measure of how much hardware demand the agreement could generate.\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) produced $89 billion in data-center revenue last quarter, accounting for roughly 92.5% of total sales. The GuruFocus chart reinforces the company's exceptional profitability and growth, alongside strong financial strength and healthy momentum, while GF Value remains the obvious weak spot. Firmus expands Nvidia's reach into another major AI-infrastructure project, but 900 megawatts measures power capacitynot Nvidia revenueso the real prize will depend on processor volumes, deployment timing and the economics behind the partnership.\n\n### meta-stock-interest gnews:fe5a897258f58328337b5c30 \n / Meta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta Platforms META launched about $35 billion of concurrent public offerings to help fund its AI build-out, made up of $22.5 billion of Class A common stock sold through forward sale agreements and about $12.5 billion of mandatory convertible preferred depositary shares. Meta shares rose 1.15% in premarket.\nNvidia NVDA , Advanced Micro Devices AMD and Broadcom AVGO have indicated non-binding interest in buying up to an aggregate $5.0 billion of the Class A stock on the same terms as other buyers. Meta, which expects $135 billion to $145 billion of capital expenditures in 2026, said it will fund its investments through a mix of operating cash flow, investment-grade debt, strategic partnerships and equity.\nMeta also plans a roughly $60 billion at-the-market program in its new Class C common stock, expected to start in the first quarter of 2027 once those shares begin trading. The preferred shares will convert automatically into common stock around August 15, 2029, and the forward sales are expected to settle by November 19, 2027. Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs are lead bookrunners.\n\n### germany-startups nvblog:germany-wants-to-lead-the-world-in-industrial-ai-how-nvidia-will-help \n German Chancellor Friedrich Merz and NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang discussed how to merge traditional German engineering with state-of-the-art AI.\nIn the cavernous hall of Kraftwerk, a former power station in Berlin, German Chancellor Friedrich Merz and NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang sat down to discuss Germany’s AI roadmap, and in particular, the industrial applications of AI.\nHuang opened by tracing NVIDIA’s longstanding relationship with German industry, beginning with gaming systems, workstations and automotive design in the early 2000s and leading to today’s partnerships in autonomous vehicle development and digital twins for manufacturing.\nThe conversation focused on how Germany can make the most of the AI revolution by playing to its strengths in manufacturing and automotive — and by encouraging AI adoption across the country’s ecosystem of small and medium-sized companies.\nEarlier in the day, as part of the Invest in Germany Summit, Chancellor Merz, the Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy and the Federal Ministry for Research, Technology and Space signed a Joint Declaration of Intent with NVIDIA.\nThe German federal government’s aim is to merge traditional German engineering and manufacturing expertise with cutting-edge research and AI technology. The declaration sets out an agreement to work on a joint initiative with NVIDIA to advance cutting edge AI models for physical AI.\nFormalizing and expanding NVIDIA’s collaboration with Germany’s federal government and its “Industry 4.0 Platform,” which is comprised of representatives from politics, industry, research and trade associations, helps achieve the government’s stated goal to “systematically bring industrial AI into widespread use and to establish a state-of-the-art industrial AI ecosystem in Europe.”\nMerz and Huang dug into industrial AI and how NVIDIA can help Germany lead the way. The German Chancellor first laid out his government’s ambition.\n“We are fully committed to be one of the most advanced places in the world, not just in the European Union, but in the world to work with AI, to integrate AI into our industry,” Merz said. “I’m really optimistic that we will be one of the leading, if not the leading, country in the world in terms of the spread of AI for all the companies — our manufacturers, machinery, automotive industry and suppliers of the automotive industry. I think this is a great opportunity.”\n“The biggest advantage we have in Germany is our so-called Mittelstand,” Merz said. “Please open your doors for AI to make use of your data. This is a real secret and a real advantage for Germany to make use of all the data which is already available in our companies.”\nHuang agreed, and praised Germany’s world-class expertise in manufacturing companies both large and small, as well as its math and science excellence. That national expertise and knowledge is what sets the German economy apart, he said, and AI will only amplify it.\n“That knowledge belongs to you,” Huang said. “It cannot be replaced. AI can only augment your capability, it will not replace your capability. Take advantage of the technology. It’s a new instrument.”\nGermany’s expertise with physical technologies is a differentiator, Huang and Merz agreed, and a powerful accelerator for the German economy when AI is included.\n“If we are successful in bringing these two aspects and parts of the business of the future together,” Merz said. “I’m really very optimistic that Germany can be one of the most successful economies in the world.”\n“This industrial revolution is so mighty, so powerful,” Huang said. “In the Chancellor’s words, you are re-engineering Germany. This is your chance to do it well.”\n“I would like to see him regularly, let’s say at least once a year, and discuss the progress we made,” Merz said, “And see a guy sitting in my office being extremely happy about Germany as a place to invest.”\nHuang also announced new investments in four German startups, part of a nearly $10 billion commitment by NVIDIA to support companies, universities, research institutions and more across Europe.\nNVIDIA has been investing across Germany’s startup ecosystem, supporting leading technology companies including n8n, Neura Robotics and Black Forest Labs .\nAgile Robots, a Munich-based robotics company, builds AI-powered robots, from robotic arms to its Agile ONE humanoid, for manufacturing, logistics and healthcare.\nAvelios Medical, a Munich-based healthcare technology startup, develops an AI-native hospital information system that serves as the core system of record for clinical and administrative workflows, integrating AI directly into hospital operations to free up doctors and nurses to deliver better patient care.\nMirelo , a German startup, develops AI models that generate sound effects, speech and music for video, helping creators and production teams produce high-quality audio that perfectly matches their visuals on screen.\nReflex Aerospace, a Berlin-based space scaleup, designs and builds high-performance satellites ready for launch in about 12 months instead of years.\nEarlier in the day, Siemens announced it’s partnering with NVIDIA to advance AI-driven, autonomous factories. Siemens showcased new AI solutions at its Erlangen factory , advancing its role as a global blueprint for autonomous manufacturing. Fully virtual production ramp-up reduces engineering effort by up to 85%, while agent-based production planning enables more adaptive manufacturing and cost savings of up to 20%.\nNVIDIA researchers used JUPITER, Europe’s largest supercomputer at the Jülich Supercomputing Centre, to run a record-breaking simulation of a fault-tolerant quantum computer. The 104-qubit simulation is the largest and most detailed of its kind to date.\n\n### residual-value-guarantee gnews:7d169a86de25d7f015c049de \n Follow the money behind the AI buildout with our AI Compute, Capital and Markets Model - understand who is funding the expansion, how deals are structured, and where the risks sit. Contact our team here to find out more.\nWe’re also hiring for SemiAnalysis’s Compute, Capital & Markets team. We have four openings across New York and Singapore:\nSenior Credit Markets Specialist - New York: 5-7 years in credit sales, sales trading, or trading, with strong pricing knowledge and investor relationships.\nCredit Analyst - New York: 5-7 years in credit analysis, ideally covering high-yield, distressed, or complex debt.\nTwo Junior Credit Analysts - New York and Singapore: 0-3 years of experience, with strong financial modeling skills. Recent graduates are welcome; prior specialization in credit, semiconductors, or AI is not required.\nIf you want to combine financial analysis with a detailed understanding of the technology and economics behind AI infrastructure, we’d like to hear from you - apply here .\nAn expanded view of Nvidia’s Balance Sheet\nAs of 2Q F1/27 (second quarter of fiscal year ended Jan 2027) Nvidia in its 10-Q disclosed $530B of gross off-balance sheet guarantees across six different line items – a jump from $184B of obligations disclosed in the prior quarter due mainly to moves in three items:\nSupply and capacity commitments went from $119B to $279B, primarily memory as explained by the CFO, with 96% of these supply commitments due by F1/29.\nGuarantees and LPS Guarantees went from $3.5B to $108.5B on SB Energy’s PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio, representing 4.25 GW leased to OpenAI for twenty years with Nvidia guaranteeing the land, power and shell.\nTwo line items appeared for the first time: $36B of AI cloud agreements, the take-or-pay floors under Neocloud capacity, and $20B of datacenter leases Nvidia has signed as a tenant but expects to reassign to third parties.\nThe $530B of commitments dwarf on-balance sheet liabilities of $91B, which includes $33.4B of total debt with $25B of senior notes issued in the most recent quarter. On the asset side, Nvidia’s cash balance has increased from $12B to $22B in the past few quarters, but its investment book (marketable and non-marketable securities) has leapt from $45B in 2Q F1/26 to $128B by 2Q F1/27. The $99B equity book generated $23.7B of gains in the first half, and $25B more of equity investments are committed.\nThe question on everyone’s mind is this: given Nvidia’s on-balance sheet and off-balance sheet obligations, what is its capacity to meet all of these commitments in the case of an industry slowdown or downturn, and how much can Nvidia grow these obligations over time while still enjoying a margin of safety? How much support does the industry need and how much support should Nvidia offer?\nIn the chart below, we illustrate the history of these off-balance sheet items as well as our estimates of how much additional obligations Nvidia can reasonably support given its balance sheet and future cash flows. To be clear, this does not represent our base case or a definitive forecast for where we think Nvidia will go with its various backstops. Rather it focuses on the extent of Nvidia’s ability to support obligations given its business profile, and we argue given this considerable firepower, that it should have the willingness to continue expanding these commitments significantly.\nWhat is obvious from the diagram above is that Nvidia is a true behemoth when it comes to cash generation – consensus estimates have it generating ~$441B of EBITDA in F1/28, and this is why we reflect this via a growth of the cash balance to $1.4T by F1/31 – though in reality Nvidia will most likely reinvest this cash into various securities. These are huge numbers, yet the scale of overall AI investment is larger – we estimate ~$11T of cumulative capex from CY24 through CY29!. This note will explore how far Nvidia can take each form of support, and what impact this could make on the massive AI investment needs ahead of us.\nWhy all the Guarantees and Backstops?\nThe mechanism that gives rise to these off-balance sheet guarantees and backstops is the same one we described in The Front End Gets Crowded (institutional article). The Gigascalers (Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Meta, Oracle) have been gatekeeping the investment grade capital that the AI buildout runs on and have benefitted tremendously from the high spread between offtake price they are being charged, and spot/short-term price that they are selling at as well as services that sit on top. In response, Nvidia has been manufacturing alternative credit anchors so Neoclouds and Neolabs can raise money without the Gigascalers. In these support arrangements, Nvidia sells GPUs at full margin on day one, then floors the buyer’s revenue (in the case of the AI Cloud Partner (AICP) Program), or guarantees the buyer’s landlord, or signs the lease itself, all so that non IG-rated operators can effectively borrow at IG rated pricing.\nHeads, demand holds and Nvidia wins twice, once on the GPU sale at full margin and again on the revenue share above the floor. Tails, demand falls and the Neocloud makes no money but remains solvent, as the revenue floor is set high enough to repay lenders. Nvidia’s backstops and guarantees also act as a breakwater against further contagion as lenders have some shielding from a downturn. Yet how high and broad the breakwater that is the Nvidia backstop/guarantee depends on Nvidia’s ability to meet these obligations.\nRisk reward is asymmetric in Nvidia’s favor as long as its balance sheet remains strong. Nvidia loses only if the backstopped Neoclouds fail to meet their lease and offtake commitments at the same time that Nvidia’s own business and cash generation slow, degrading its ability to make good on these obligations. The two are correlated, since a Neocloud that cannot pay its lease is a Neocloud that has stopped buying GPUs. Only if a downcycle is steep enough to overwhelm Nvidia’s ability to meet its off-balance sheet obligations, then it will be tails, we all lose.\nBy our count, Nvidia today backstops ~6.5 GW of capacity, most of which is not yet built. Our datacenter model has Microsoft, Meta, AWS and Oracle leasing roughly 15 GW of third-party capacity in 2026 and we expect them to lease more than 35 GW by 2028. Each of those leases typically run fifteen to twenty years, and developers borrow against them with investment grade pricing. The Gigascalers are the implicit backstop of the buildout and are in aggregate far larger force than Nvidia’s backstopped capacity, yet Nvidia’s program gets the attention because it is new and because many investors have Cisco PTSD, contracted vicariously without an appreciation for the dissimilarities.\nWe will now explore how this support could evolve in detail – walking the six off-balance sheet obligation lines in the recent quarter’s disclosure, plus a new one we have created to track residual value guarantees under the capital partnership.\nThis line item made its debut in the 2Q F1/27 earnings report released on August 26th, 2026 at $36B of aggregate obligation, spread across six years. It represents Nvidia’s AI Cloud Partner (AICP) program, where Nvidia backstops GPU rental contracts for six years at a below market rate, approximately $2.35/hr/GPU on average for the GB300 vs the $4.50 to $4.60 range for 5y contracts, with the intent of making these AI clusters bankable. Neoclouds in this program would then seek to rent out compute on shorter term contracts to AI Natives and other tenants, but can always rent to Nvidia at any time should these contracts fall through or not renew. We published an extensive analysis of this structure in a July 2026 Newsletter Article .\nThere are three deals announced so far under AICP: Firmus’s 360MW deal in Batam corresponds to a $21.1B total obligation, the SharonAI deal means $4.2B of obligations and we estimate the GMI deal corresponds to $2.2B of obligations for Nvidia. We believe there are still a few deals that have been completed before Nvidia reportedly paused AICP two weeks ago and estimate this line item will close F1/27 at $51B.\nWe do understand that Nvidia is not advancing any further AICP deals at this time, but believe that they still wish to be supportive in one form or another. In this analysis, our forward modeling still assumes considerable growth in this line item to reflect our view that Nvidia should offer support in some form, as well as to explore how far Nvidia could go in terms of explicit support.\nResidual Value Guarantees under Capital Partnerships\nEarlier in August 2026, Nvidia announced its partnership with a number of private equity funds to mobilize over $500B of third-party capital to support the buildout of AI Clusters. Under this program, institutional investors are in the driver’s seat and will evaluate and price each proposed project, with the only support from Nvidia being a residual value guarantee for up to 25% of a deal. In his X post detailing the program , Jensen Huang highlighted that this support would be limited and on a project-by-project basis. Though there are few details on what such a program could look like, we drew inspiration from the Google-Broadcom-Anthropic-Apollo TPU structure to surmise how deals could be structured.\nIn the TPU backstop structure, if Anthropic is unable to continue to service lease payments to the TPU SPV, then the structure can be resolved by Broadcom assuming the lease or by the racks being sold. If the racks are sold, then the residual value guarantee takes effect, as Broadcom must then make the senior noteholders whole after such equipment sale – effectively plugging the residual value gap, if any. The deal gets better – Google will play its part by also backstopping rental payments to the various datacenter operators. The effect is that lenders to the TPU SPV are effectively taking Broadcom credit risk while lenders to the datacenters are taking Google credit risk.\nNvidia’s proposed structure would appear to wean investors away from this dual backstop as it not only reduces the residual value guarantee to only up to 25%, but it also is silent on any datacenter backstop.\nWe think that the funds could tranche the lending to create different pools of risk, with the first loss being taken by riskier equity/mezzanine style lending and the last loss taken by more senior creditors. Nvidia’s 25% residual value guarantee would insert itself above the senior lenders but after the equity/mezzanine investors, adding a meaningful amount of crumple room to the structure, which could place the senior tranche at low enough risk of actual shortfall to attract conservative lenders like banks.\nThis structure can also provide the most bang for buck when it comes to Nvidia’s off-balance sheet obligations. While the AICP creates $59B of obligations per GW of capacity enabled and the PORTS-Pike structure requires $25B of obligations per GW, the residual value structure would only require $9.4B of obligations per GW enabled, though the datacenter leg still needs to be solved for.\nWhile Nvidia has said the program targets over $500B of capital, we think it will likely swell larger due to the strong compute demand. In the spirit of examining the full extent of Nvidia’s maneuverability, we take an aspirational stance, assuming $2.5T of total funding under this program through F1/31, with the obligations under this line item ending F1/27 at $55B before growing to $373B by F1/31.\n\n### spacex-chip-financing gnews:ba8c6c8c0a4e120c799e30c2 \n SpaceX Seeks $40 Billion to Fund Nvidia Chip Purchases, Led by Apollo — FT\nSpaceX is moving forward with a $40 billion (approximately ¥6.3 trillion) capital raise to fund purchases of Nvidia semiconductors. The plan calls for roughly $10 billion in bank loans and $30 billion in investment-grade bonds, with Apollo Global Management leading the effort and PIMCO among the institutions in talks to provide financing. The transaction is expected to close in 2027, though discussions remain in early stages. The company, which only completed its Nasdaq listing and inaugural bond issuance in June, is accelerating capital markets activity to fund massive investments in AI computing infrastructure — underscoring the intensifying competition for AI semiconductors.\nSpaceX, the U.S. space exploration company, is advancing a capital raise of approximately $40 billion (about ¥6.3 trillion) to fund purchases of Nvidia semiconductors, the Financial Times reported on October 6, citing multiple people familiar with the matter.\nThe financing is expected to be led by Apollo Global Management, with plans to secure roughly $10 billion (about ¥1.6 trillion) through bank loans and $30 billion (about ¥4.7 trillion) through investment-grade bond issuance. Apollo is said to be assisting with the sale of bonds to a broad range of investors. According to sources familiar with the discussions, talks remain in early stages and the transaction could still fall through.\nPIMCO, the major bond manager, is among the financial institutions in discussions to provide funding. The transaction is expected to close in 2027.\nIn August, Apollo joined a lending framework for AI computing infrastructure that Nvidia launched with six major asset managers. The initiative aims to mobilize more than $500 billion in third-party capital to fund Nvidia chip purchases for customer companies.\nSpaceX, Apollo, Nvidia, and PIMCO did not immediately respond to Reuters requests for comment.\nAccording to Bloomberg, SpaceX shares (SPCX) fell 1.2% to $169.79 in U.S. after-hours trading on October 6 following the report. Nvidia shares were roughly flat.\nSpaceX, led by Elon Musk, continues to grow rapidly through its Starlink satellite communications service and rocket development. The company is reportedly pursuing large-scale procurement of Nvidia GPUs to expand its AI-related computing capacity.\nSpaceX listed on the Nasdaq in June 2026 and issued $25 billion (about ¥3.9 trillion) in inaugural investment-grade bonds that same month. The weighted average interest rate was 5.855% with an average maturity of 11.7 years. Moody's, S&P, and Fitch all assigned investment-grade ratings (Baa1, BBB, and BBB+, respectively), each with a \"stable\" outlook. The new investment-grade bond offering would exceed the initial $25 billion issuance in size.\nDebt-fueled fundraising for AI computing infrastructure has spread across the industry. Nvidia issued $25 billion in bonds in June, Meta issued $30 billion in October 2025, Oracle plans to raise up to $50 billion in 2026, and Amazon has borrowed more than $80 billion since early 2025. SpaceX itself made capital expenditures of approximately $18.4 billion in the April–June 2026 quarter, of which roughly $15.8 billion went toward AI computing infrastructure.\n$10 billion bank loans + $30 billion investment-grade bonds\nSpaceX's satellite business is also gaining prominence in the Japanese telecommunications market. SoftBank and NTT Docomo are advancing direct satellite-to-device collaborations with the company, and in August, KDDI became the first in Asia to sign a service agreement for the next-generation \"Starlink Mobile V2\" satellite, aiming to launch voice call services in 2027.\nNvidia semiconductors have become the de facto standard for AI computing, with demand continuing to far outstrip supply. SpaceX's large-scale procurement highlights the intensifying competition among companies for AI infrastructure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))' | rg -n -A 50 -B 15 '问题怎么写|同一题、续期|绑定|约束力' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))' | rg -n -A 50 -B 15 '问题怎么写|同一题、续期|绑定|约束力' \"","aggregated_output":"707-D243 第 2 条\n708-。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开\n709-D191 第 3 条\n710-。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定\n711-D191 第 1 条、D195\n712-。\n713-\n714-\n715-开题理由是封闭的\n716-D184\n717-：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。\n718-\n719-\n720-\n721-\n722:问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写\n723-\n724-\n725-\n726-\n727-一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开\n728-PRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新\n729-，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。\n730-\n731-\n732-写成问句，不写成断言\n733-PRD 06 Rationale\n734-。\n735-\n736-\n737-结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档\n738-PRD 06 梯子第 2 条\n739-。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏\n740-PRD 06、D186\n741-。\n742-\n743-\n744-到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年\n745-PRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185\n746-。\n747-\n748-\n749-\n750-\n751-4\n752-开题标准\n753-\n754-\n755-一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。\n756-开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标\n757-第 1 版 §6；评审 §1\n758-。\n759-\n760-\n761:颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判\n762-D248\n763-。\n764-\n765-\n766-另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开\n767-D201\n768-。\n769-\n770-\n771-四个测试\n772-第 1 版 §6\n773-\n774-\n775-\n776-\n777-值得追\n778-写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\n779-D248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品\n780-；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。\n781-\n782-\n783-独立意义\n784-能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据\n785-评审 §1 第 3、8 条\n786-。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。\n787-\n788-\n789-能核对\n790-有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。\n791-\n792-\n793-不是重复\n794-和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库\n795-D211\n796-，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。\n797-\n798-\n799-\n800-\n801-另外三条写法检查\n802-评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条\n803-。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。\n804-\n805-\n806-\n807-\n808-原文支持：\n809-主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天\n810-D185、D210\n811-。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。\n--\n874-证据\n875-\n876-\n877-后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外）\n878-PRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D185\n879-证据\n880-\n881-\n882-同一个量的另一个估计、另一种口径、另一个来源\n883-第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n884-那个已开问题的证据，由匹配并入\n885-\n886-\n887-\n888-\n889:同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的\n890-\n891-\n892-\n893-\n894-总规则：\n895-同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题\n896-D248\n897-。\n898-\n899-\n900-同一题：\n901-同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向\n902-第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n903-；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日\n904-PRD 06\n905-。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。\n906-\n907-\n908-同一个计划的几种说法\n909-（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题\n910-D248 第 1 条\n911-。\n912-\n913-\n914-同一件事的不同截止日：\n915-早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章\n916-D248 第 2 条\n917-。\n918-\n919-\n920-同一代产品的几个里程碑：\n921-一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\n922-D248 第 3 条\n923-。\n924-\n925-\n926-公司自己的量：\n927-只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点\n928-D248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\n929-。\n930-\n931-\n932-身份：\n933-事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对\n934-D248 第 5 条\n935-。\n936-\n937-\n938-续期：\n939-同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间\n--\n966-\n967-\n968-\n969-\n970-谁判，谁查\n971-\n972-\n973-\n974-\n975-内容\n976-谁\n977-\n978-\n979-\n980-\n981:测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写\n982-抽取的模型（判断）\n983-\n984-\n985-结果档形状、期间是真日期、到期在文章之后、季度数、年限、约束性义务 18 个月、\n986-settled_by\n987- 不为空\n988-代码：只查形状，不判重要性\n989-D154、D155、D184\n990-\n991-\n992:和库里的同一题、续期、另一题\n993-匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决\n994-D190\n995-；共同量由代码\n996-D197\n997-\n998-\n999-\n1000-\n1001-年限按公司财年数：2026 年 7 月时 NVIDIA 在 FY2027（2027 年 1 月结束），所以 FY2028 是明年。代码原来按自然年算，把 FY2028 当成后年拒掉；在 v29 答案的 40 个带年份的候选里拒错了 2 个。已修，测试是 \n1002-tests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\n1003-D243\n1004-。\n1005-\n1006-\n1007-5\n1008-证据标准\n1009-\n1010-\n1011-5.1 留的规则和四类\n1012-\n1013-\n1014-一个在追这家公司的分析员，读到会在本周笔记里写下来，因为它让某个问题的答案更可能或更不可能。\n1015-问题不限于库里已有的，也不限于本公司：客户、供应商、竞争者、监管、宏观都算\n1016-第 1 版 §2；D208 七类驱动\n1017-。\n1018-\n1019-\n1020-整体倾向谨慎，对增量敏感。\n1021-杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索。判一句话之前先问：这是新信息吗？它让哪个问题的答案变了？一件新发生的事、一个新数字、一个新的具体判断，是增量，不能漏；重复常识、描述长期状况、泛泛的看法，是杂音\n1022-D243\n1023-。增量和杂音的具体界线按 5.3 各行和边界一起判\n1024-D250\n1025-。\n1026-\n1027-\n1028-从第一段读到最后一段，分析员会记的（按本节和 5.3 各行边界一起判）都留。一页有八条就是八条，不挑\n1029-D199\n1030-。它们通常是下面四类之一；四类描述的是会记的陈述长什么样，不是\"属于四类就一定留\"，留不留由上面的规则判；具体不留的行和边界见 5.3。\n1031-\n1032-\n1033-\n1034-\n1035-公司自己的数字、指引或决定\n1036-第 1 版 §2\n1037-\"同店销售本季增长 4.2%\"；\"管理层把全年收入指引定在 120–130 亿美元\"；\"董事会批准 100 亿美元回购\"；\"公司取消软件订阅费，生产使用免费\"\n1038-Alex 判 8，Omniverse\n1039-。\n1040-\n1041-\n1042-客户、供应商、竞争者、伙伴的有规模、有日期或有名字的动作，以及它们公布的数字\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The existing library already tracks the Hugging Face acquisition and the totals for credit support and equity commitments. I’m checking the article text carefully where a headline or a passing reference could make a deal appear more specific than it is."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story, x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story in [\\\"firmus-investment\\\",\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\",\\\"germany-startups\\\"] for x in c[story][:3]]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story, x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story in [\\\"firmus-investment\\\",\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\",\\\"germany-startups\\\"] for x in c[story][:3]]' \"","aggregated_output":"\n### firmus-investment gnews:e68d5b0219b8755050bcdaf1 \n Nvidia-backed Firmus secured a multiyear agreement to provide OpenAI with computing capacity from two Malaysian data centers. Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator powerhouse supplying Firmus's next-generation infrastructure, fell approximately 1.5% to $226.86 in Tuesday morning trading.\nThe OpenAI deal pushes Firmus's committed customer capacity beyond 900 megawatts. The company currently operates two facilities and is building five more across the Asia-Pacific region, with future sites set to deploy Nvidia's Vera Rubin processors at scale. Firmus did not reveal the contract's financial terms, leaving investors without a clear measure of how much hardware demand the agreement could generate.\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) produced $89 billion in data-center revenue last quarter, accounting for roughly 92.5% of total sales. The GuruFocus chart reinforces the company's exceptional profitability and growth, alongside strong financial strength and healthy momentum, while GF Value remains the obvious weak spot. Firmus expands Nvidia's reach into another major AI-infrastructure project, but 900 megawatts measures power capacitynot Nvidia revenueso the real prize will depend on processor volumes, deployment timing and the economics behind the partnership.\n\n### firmus-investment gnews:f3b48b526570bd03cc87ab30 \n OpenAI has signed an infrastructure deal that could boost demand for Nvidia's ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) next-generation AI systems across Asia.\nAustralia-based Firmus will provide OpenAI computing capacity from two Malaysian data centers under a multiyear contract. As anchor customer, OpenAI gives the projects committed demand before capacity is deployed.\nFirmus plans to install Nvidia's Vera Rubin processors across its Asia-Pacific data centers. The deal lifts Firmus' contracted capacity above 900 megawatts.\nFirmus operates two AI data centers in Australia and Singapore, with five more under development. Valued above $10.5 billion, Firmus counts Nvidia, Jane Street, Blackstone funds and Coatue Management among its backers.\nThe contract value was not disclosed. Investors should therefore treat it as a potential demand catalyst, not confirmed Nvidia revenue or a specific order.\nWhy the OpenAI Deal Matters for Nvidia Stock\nAnchor customers make infrastructure projects easier to finance because they reduce uncertainty about future utilization. OpenAI's commitment could therefore help Firmus advance its Malaysian facilities and ultimately purchase more Vera Rubin systems.\nThe arrangement also demonstrates that AI computing demand is spreading beyond established U.S. data-center regions. Malaysia offers space and power for new capacity, potentially expanding Nvidia's geographic customer base.\nNvidia already has a larger agreement with OpenAI to deploy at least 10 gigawatts of systems for next-generation infrastructure. The first gigawatt is targeted for the second half of 2026 and will use Vera Rubin technology.\nThe opportunity carries execution risks. Malaysia's data-center expansion faces scrutiny over electricity and water consumption, potentially affecting approvals, construction schedules and operating costs.\nFor Nvidia investors, the key questions are when Firmus orders hardware, how quickly facilities open and whether OpenAI utilizes contracted capacity. Those milestones would convert an encouraging agreement into tangible Nvidia revenue.\n\n### firmus-investment nvidianews:nvidia-expands-ai-infrastructure-capacity-in-partnership-with-australias-data-center-ecosystem \n NVIDIA Expands AI Infrastructure Capacity in Partnership With Australia’s Data Center Ecosystem | NVIDIA Newsroom\nNVIDIA Expands AI Infrastructure Capacity in Partnership With Australia’s Data Center Ecosystem\nFirmus, Sharon AI, IREN, Megaport, ResetData, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk to Deliver Up to 2-Gigawatt Buildout by 2027, Meeting Surging Demand From AI Labs and AI-Native Startups\nSeptember 9, 2026\nNVIDIA's Endeavor building in Santa Clara, Calif.\nNVIDIA is collaborating with Australian NVIDIA Cloud Partners and AI infrastructure partners including Firmus, Sharon AI, IREN, ResetData, Megaport, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk to expand land, power and shell capacity designed to host multiple generations of NVIDIA DSX compute infrastructure.\nBuilt on the NVIDIA DSX platform, these full-stack AI factories provide high-performance AI compute for Australian startups, universities, researchers, enterprises, developers and AI-native companies.\nExpanded access to NVIDIA accelerated computing and NVIDIA Nemotron open models will enable organizations including Heidi and Atlassian to build regional AI models, applications and agents for industries nationwide.\nNVIDIA today announced that it is collaborating with a growing ecosystem of Australian NVIDIA Cloud Partners (NCPs) and AI infrastructure partners to expand land, power and shell capacity designed to host multiple generations of NVIDIA DSX™ AI factories, supporting the nation’s growing demand for AI compute — with up to a 2-gigawatt buildout by 2027.\nThe participating providers will operate the AI factories, with NVIDIA delivering the DSX platform, accelerated computing, networking, software and ecosystem support.\n“AI factories turn energy into intelligence — the essential resource of the AI economy,” said Raj Mirpuri, vice president of global AI clouds and infrastructure ecosystem at NVIDIA. “NVIDIA DSX is a full-stack platform compatible with the CUDA ecosystem and enhanced through software over the life of the infrastructure, making AI factories more productive, fungible and durable — and a new investable asset class. Through our partnerships with Australian NCPs, this infrastructure can support multiple generations of NVIDIA compute and expand local access to accelerated computing and NVIDIA Nemotron open models, helping Australian innovators build AI at home.”\nGrowing AI adoption across industries is increasing demand for high-performance computing. Expanding capacity in Australia will enable local innovators to develop models, applications and agents while supporting additional power generation projects to support regional and global demand for AI compute.\nNVIDIA DSX is a full-stack AI factory platform spanning facilities infrastructure, computing, networking, software and reference designs. Compatible with the NVIDIA CUDA ® ecosystem and enhanced through software over the life of infrastructure, DSX helps operators serve a broad range of workloads and customers, improve performance and utilization, and extend the useful life of their assets — strengthening productivity, fungibility, durability, rentability and long-term economics.\nThis expanded capacity will give Australian innovators greater access to accelerated computing and NVIDIA Nemotron™ open models to develop locally relevant models, applications and agents, while supporting regional and global demand for AI compute.\nAustralia’s established sustainable energy and digital infrastructure, deep technology talent and reputation as a trusted global economic partner provide a strong foundation for startups, universities, researchers, enterprises, developers and AI-native companies to train, deploy and scale AI.\nBuilding Australia’s AI Factory Infrastructure\nAcross Australia, NCPs and AI infrastructure partners Firmus, Sharon AI, IREN, Megaport, ResetData, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk are already investing in NVIDIA DSX AI factories to deliver high-performance AI compute to organizations nationwide.\nFirmus is expanding its Project Southgate initiative, developing NVIDIA-powered AI factories designed to support hyperscale and AI-native demand across Australia and Asia-Pacific.\n“Project Southgate represents a tangible commitment to building Australia’s AI future,” said Oliver Curtis, cofounder and co-CEO of Firmus. “Together with NVIDIA, we are developing AI factories that will give Australian businesses, researchers and innovators access to world-class computing capacity at home, while positioning Australia to meet growing AI demand across Asia-Pacific.”\nSharon AI is deploying up to 68,000 NVIDIA GPUs connected with NVIDIA Quantum InfiniBand and NVIDIA Spectrum-X™ Ethernet networking, using the NVIDIA DSX platform to support startups, enterprises and research organizations.\n“ Australia has the energy, connectivity and ambition to lead in AI. That potential only becomes real capability when organizations can access world-class compute without compromising on security or sovereignty,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI. “By building on NVIDIA’s DSX platform, Sharon AI is equipped to bring gigawatt-scale AI factories to Australian enterprises, researchers, government and AI natives. With the trust and reliability needed to build, train and deploy AI at scale, Australia can turn its natural advantages into a genuine national AI capability.”\nIREN is partnering with NVIDIA to accelerate deployment of large-scale AI factories by combining NVIDIA’s DSX AI factory architecture with IREN’s expertise across power, land, data centers, GPU deployment and infrastructure operations.\n“Building AI infrastructure at scale requires integrating every layer: data centers, compute and software,” said Daniel Roberts, cofounder and co-CEO of IREN. “Combining IREN’s vertically integrated platform with the NVIDIA DSX AI factory reference architecture provides a repeatable blueprint to bring capacity online at scale. We are applying that blueprint across our development portfolio, bringing IREN’s proven international delivery and operating experience to our 800-megawatt Bundey campus in South Australia.”\nMegaport is expanding access to NVIDIA accelerated computing through its wholly owned subsidiary, Latitude.sh, to support the inference needs of leading AI enterprises. Combined with Megaport’s global software-defined network, Latitude.sh compute platform brings AI processing closer to users, reducing latency and improving responsiveness.\n“Megaport is proud to support Australia’s growing AI infrastructure ecosystem as an Australian-founded global infrastructure provider,” said Michael Reid, CEO of Megaport. “By investing in NVIDIA AI factories and expanding access to high-performance AI infrastructure, we help Australian organisations build and deploy AI locally, and scale globally through Megaport’s worldwide platform.”\nResetData is building AI infrastructure in Australia, providing NVIDIA accelerated computing through its AI cloud platform for enterprises, government and research customers that require high-performance compute and Australian data residency.\n“At ResetData, we’ve proven that when Australian businesses and researchers like the team at ProCan at the Children’s Medical Research Institute have access to high-performance compute, they can deliver industry-leading results,” said Marcel Zalloua, joint CEO of ResetData. “That is why we are proud to partner with NVIDIA and develop the sovereign, purpose-built infrastructure solutions that will accelerate Australian expertise and empower local businesses with the tools needed to compete and innovate on the global stage.”\nCDC has developed AI-ready data center infrastructure across Australia and New Zealand to support high-density AI and high-performance computing workloads. Its facilities feature advanced cooling technologies, including direct liquid-to-chip cooling, designed to support the deployment of NVIDIA accelerated computing infrastructure at scale.\n“CDC operates more than 550 megawatts of capacity across Australia and New Zealand, with a further 800 megawatts under construction and additional growth planned to support world-leading, high-density workloads for critical infrastructure,” said Greg Boorer, founder and CEO of CDC. “Our facilities use 100% renewable electricity and incorporate pioneering zero water advanced cooling technologies certified for NVIDIA accelerated computing infrastructure.”\nNEXTDC has developed AI-ready data center infrastructure designed to support high-density AI factories, providing the power, cooling, connectivity and physical infrastructure required to deploy increasingly compute-intensive AI systems.\n“NEXTDC operates and continues to develop liquid-cooled, high-density infrastructure, including AI factories built on NVIDIA DSX and interconnected with the world’s leading cloud platforms,” said David Dzienciol, chief customer and commercial officer at NEXTDC.\nAirTrunk is expanding AI-ready powered shells across the region, including direct-to-chip liquid-cooling infrastructure designed to support high-density AI workloads.\n“AI is reshaping hyperscale infrastructure in Australia, driving new approaches to location, capacity, cooling, power and connectivity,” said Damien Spillane, chief customer and innovation officer of AirTrunk. “AirTrunk is innovating to meet this demand while creating skilled expertise, capability and economic value in Australia.”\nFrom AI Infrastructure to AI Innovation\nGreater access to accelerated computing is enabling Australia’s developers, AI-native companies and enterprises to build models, applications and agents tailored to local industries and communities.\nOrganizations including Heidi and Atlassian are using NVIDIA Nemotron open models, datasets and tools to build and customize AI across language, reasoning, speech and multimodal applications.\nHeidi combines NVIDIA Nemotron models with Heidi’s specialized clinical knowledge to redefine voice-driven AI to establish a new benchmark for healthcare excellence.\n“NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra gives us the flexibility to build and post-train models for the real complexity of healthcare,” said Yu Liu, cofounder and chief technology officer of Heidi. “Combining Nemotron with Heidi’s clinical training and fine-tuning capabilities helps us reason across patient histories, clinical context and guidelines at scale, turning that information into actionable insights while preserving the trust and privacy patients expect.”\nAtlassian is advancing Rovo’s semantic search with NVIDIA AI to make enterprise search more intelligent, context-aware and capable of surfacing more relevant insights across complex data and workflows.\n“NVIDIA’s accelerated computing infrastructure and open model ecosystem are foundational to how Atlassian builds and delivers AI,” said Guihong Cao, head of engineering for Rovo + AI at Atlassian. “From supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning to powering intelligent experiences in Rovo, NVIDIA provides the performance, economics and flexibility we need to innovate responsibly at the scale our customers expect. As Australia’s AI infrastructure continues to grow, it will play an increasingly important role in that journey, and we’re excited to keep pushing the boundaries of what’s possible together.”\nTogether, these efforts connect AI infrastructure with the open models and software that turn computing capacity into applications. By expanding access across the full AI stack, NVIDIA and Australia’s AI ecosystem are enabling innovators across research, industry and the public sector to build and scale AI locally and bring Australian innovation to the world.\nCertain statements in this press release including, but not limited to, statements as to: AI infrastructure being able to serve every industry and create lasting value, making it a new investable asset class; expectations with respect to demand for high-performance computing and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to expanding capacity in Australia; expectations with respect to growth, performance, availability, and benefits of NVIDIA’s products, services and technologies, and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to NVIDIA’s third party arrangements, including with its collaborators and partners; expectations with respect to technology developments, and related trends and drivers; projected market growth and trends; expectations with respect to AI and related industries; and other statements that are not historical facts are forward-looking statements within the meaning of Section 27A of the Securities Act of 1933, as amended, and Section 21E of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, which are subject to the “safe harbor” created by those sections based on management’s beliefs and assumptions and on information currently available to management and are subject to risks and uncertainties that could cause results to be materially different than expectations. Important factors that could cause actual results to differ materially include: global economic and political conditions; NVIDIA’s reliance on third parties to manufacture, assemble, package and test NVIDIA’s products; the impact of technological development and competition; development of new products and technologies or enhancements to NVIDIA’s existing products and technologies; market acceptance of NVIDIA’s products or NVIDIA’s partners’ products; design, manufacturing or software defects; changes in consumer preferences or demands; changes in industry standards and interfaces; unexpected loss of performance of NVIDIA’s products or technologies when integrated into systems; NVIDIA’s ability to realize the potential benefits of business investments or acquisitions; and changes in applicable laws and regulations, as well as other factors detailed from time to time in the most recent reports NVIDIA files with the Securities and Exchange Commission, or SEC, including, but not limited to, its Annual Report on Form 10-K and Quarterly Reports on Form 10-Q. Copies of reports filed with the SEC are posted on NVIDIA’s website and are available from NVIDIA without charge. These forward-looking statements are not guarantees of future performance and speak only as of the date hereof, and, except as required by law, NVIDIA disclaims any obligation to update these forward-looking statements to reflect future events or circumstances.\n© 2026 NVIDIA Corporation. All rights reserved. NVIDIA, the NVIDIA logo, CUDA, NVIDIA DSX, NVIDIA Spectrum-X and NVIDIA Nemotron are trademarks and/or registered trademarks of NVIDIA Corporation in the U.S. and/or other countries. Other company and product names may be trademarks of the respective companies with which they are associated.\nRanda Hinton\nCertain statements in this press release including, but not limited to, statements as to: AI infrastructure being able to serve every industry and create lasting value, making it a new investable asset class; expectations with respect to demand for high-performance computing and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to expanding capacity in Australia; expectations with respect to growth, performance, availability, and benefits of NVIDIA’s products, services and technologies, and related trends and drivers; expectations with respect to NVIDIA’s third party arrangements, including with its collaborators and partners; expectations with respect to technology developments, and related trends and drivers; projected market growth and trends; expectations with respect to AI and related industries; and other statements that are not historical facts are forward-looking statements within the meaning of Section 27A of the Securities Act of 1933, as amended, and Section 21E of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, which are subject to the “safe harbor” created by those sections based on management’s beliefs and assumptions and on information currently available to management and are subject to risks and uncertainties that could cause results to be materially different than expectations. Important factors that could cause actual results to differ materially include: global economic and political conditions; NVIDIA’s reliance on third parties to manufacture, assemble, package and test NVIDIA’s products; the impact of technological development and competition; development of new products and technologies or enhancements to NVIDIA’s existing products and technologies; market acceptance of NVIDIA’s products or NVIDIA’s partners’ products; design, manufacturing or software defects; changes in consumer preferences or demands; changes in industry standards and interfaces; unexpected loss of performance of NVIDIA’s products or technologies when integrated into systems; NVIDIA’s ability to realize the potential benefits of business investments or acquisitions; and changes in applicable laws and regulations, as well as other factors detailed from time to time in the most recent reports NVIDIA files with the Securities and Exchange Commission, or SEC, including, but not limited to, its Annual Report on Form 10-K and Quarterly Reports on Form 10-Q. Copies of reports filed with the SEC are posted on NVIDIA’s website and are available from NVIDIA without charge. These forward-looking statements are not guarantees of future performance and speak only as of the date hereof, and, except as required by law, NVIDIA disclaims any obligation to update these forward-looking statements to reflect future events or circumstances.\n© 2026 NVIDIA Corporation. All rights reserved. NVIDIA, the NVIDIA logo, CUDA, NVIDIA DSX, NVIDIA Spectrum-X and NVIDIA Nemotron are trademarks and/or registered trademarks of NVIDIA Corporation in the U.S. and/or other countries. Other company and product names may be trademarks of the respective companies with which they are associated.\n\n### meta-stock-interest gnews:fe5a897258f58328337b5c30 \n / Meta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta Platforms META launched about $35 billion of concurrent public offerings to help fund its AI build-out, made up of $22.5 billion of Class A common stock sold through forward sale agreements and about $12.5 billion of mandatory convertible preferred depositary shares. Meta shares rose 1.15% in premarket.\nNvidia NVDA , Advanced Micro Devices AMD and Broadcom AVGO have indicated non-binding interest in buying up to an aggregate $5.0 billion of the Class A stock on the same terms as other buyers. Meta, which expects $135 billion to $145 billion of capital expenditures in 2026, said it will fund its investments through a mix of operating cash flow, investment-grade debt, strategic partnerships and equity.\nMeta also plans a roughly $60 billion at-the-market program in its new Class C common stock, expected to start in the first quarter of 2027 once those shares begin trading. The preferred shares will convert automatically into common stock around August 15, 2029, and the forward sales are expected to settle by November 19, 2027. Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs are lead bookrunners.\n\n### meta-stock-interest gnews:0a24b607f8da809cd66f1376 \n Meta has started about $35 billion in stock offerings and has plans to commence a $60 billion market sale in 2027.\nMeta will receive about $12.5 billion upfront and will complete the $22.5 billion common-stock transaction by November 2027.\nNvidia, AMD and Broadcom have all expressed non-binding interest in buying up to $5 billion of shares.\nAccording to reports, Meta is raising $95 billion for AI. Yet, the filings show it’s a much more gradual process.\nOf the $35 billion of shares offered on 26th October, Meta will deliver about $12.5 billion upfront. The company will deliver the remaining $22.5 billion on dates it selects, but no later than 19th November 2027.\nThe other $60 billion is purely a planned offering, not a final transaction. Meta hasn’t yet signed an at-the-market program and confirms it won’t begin until early 2027. The release makes no mention of a $95 billion total – the figure is simply $35 billion plus $60 billion, as CNBC and others reported.\nMeta is issuing two types of securities. The first is $22.5 billion in Class A common stock, sold via forward sale agreements with Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs. The banks will borrow the shares and sell them now; Meta will then deliver the shares and receive payment later. The second is 250m depositary shares in a new mandatory convertible preferred stock which will convert to common stock on or about August 15, 2029. Meta intends to file for a Nasdaq listing under the ticker METAM and will determine the dividend rate at the time of pricing.\nMeta is still generating cash. Q3 revenue was up 26% to $64.6 billion, and the company reported $97 billion in operating cash flow for the first 9 months of 2026. However, spending exceeds cash generation. Q3 capex was $34.91 billion versus $32.79 billion from operations, resulting in negative free cash flow of $2.12 billion.\nMeta expects 2026 capex to be in the $135bn to $145bn range. The Globe and Mail notes that its cash and securities total $82.45 billion, just slightly more than its long-term debt of $80.92 billion.\nDebt has been the key to bridging the funding gap. In October 2025, Meta issued $30 billion in bonds. Tech Funding News reported that Pimco and Blue Owl led the $29 billion financing for Meta’s Hyperion data centre in Louisiana. TFN also reported that Meta had disclosed $279 billion of future AI-related data centre lease commitments in its Q2 filing.\nAcross Big Tech, Bloomberg reported on 10th July that AI-related debt had doubled to $350 billion. Meta says its Aa3 and AA- ratings mean it can keep issuing debt, and the new equity is an additional source of capital.\nAlphabet set the example with its June 1 announcement of $80 billion in equity: $30 billion in underwritten offerings, $40 billion in an at-the-market program, and $10 billion from Berkshire Hathaway.\nFor Meta, the suppliers could be the anchor investors. Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom have all expressed interest in buying up to $5 billion of the Class A shares on the same terms as the other investors.\nThe $5 billion is less than 4% of Meta’s 2026 capex, so the details matter more than the size of the figure. That leads straight to the old question: when Nvidia put $100bn into OpenAI in 2025, did that look like circular financing?\nHow Zuckerberg keeps control of the votes\nThe at-the-market program will issue a new share class which doesn’t currently exist.\nOn October 21, Meta announced non-voting Class C common stock, effective January 19, 2027, with a one-time dividend of one Class C share for every Class A and Class B share. Meta expects this to work like a 2-for-1 split. A special committee of independent directors said the new shares will also pay employees and fund acquisitions.\nZuck started Facebook in 2004, and as of September, 3.64 billion people use his Meta apps every day. He has about 60.6% of the voting power and a 13.4% economic interest. The sale of Class C shares doesn’t impact his voting power.\nUnder the Founder Alignment Agreement, he can sell only 20% of his Class C shares freely. To sell the remainder, he would have to sell a Class A share or convert a Class B share. The agreement also requires him to remain a California resident for 8 years.\nThe first hurdle will come before the $60 billion offering. Meta has to price a preferred share with no fixed dividend, and then after 19th January 2027 it will have to get the market to accept a new share class that has never traded before.\n\n### germany-startups nvblog:germany-wants-to-lead-the-world-in-industrial-ai-how-nvidia-will-help \n German Chancellor Friedrich Merz and NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang discussed how to merge traditional German engineering with state-of-the-art AI.\nIn the cavernous hall of Kraftwerk, a former power station in Berlin, German Chancellor Friedrich Merz and NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang sat down to discuss Germany’s AI roadmap, and in particular, the industrial applications of AI.\nHuang opened by tracing NVIDIA’s longstanding relationship with German industry, beginning with gaming systems, workstations and automotive design in the early 2000s and leading to today’s partnerships in autonomous vehicle development and digital twins for manufacturing.\nThe conversation focused on how Germany can make the most of the AI revolution by playing to its strengths in manufacturing and automotive — and by encouraging AI adoption across the country’s ecosystem of small and medium-sized companies.\nEarlier in the day, as part of the Invest in Germany Summit, Chancellor Merz, the Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy and the Federal Ministry for Research, Technology and Space signed a Joint Declaration of Intent with NVIDIA.\nThe German federal government’s aim is to merge traditional German engineering and manufacturing expertise with cutting-edge research and AI technology. The declaration sets out an agreement to work on a joint initiative with NVIDIA to advance cutting edge AI models for physical AI.\nFormalizing and expanding NVIDIA’s collaboration with Germany’s federal government and its “Industry 4.0 Platform,” which is comprised of representatives from politics, industry, research and trade associations, helps achieve the government’s stated goal to “systematically bring industrial AI into widespread use and to establish a state-of-the-art industrial AI ecosystem in Europe.”\nMerz and Huang dug into industrial AI and how NVIDIA can help Germany lead the way. The German Chancellor first laid out his government’s ambition.\n“We are fully committed to be one of the most advanced places in the world, not just in the European Union, but in the world to work with AI, to integrate AI into our industry,” Merz said. “I’m really optimistic that we will be one of the leading, if not the leading, country in the world in terms of the spread of AI for all the companies — our manufacturers, machinery, automotive industry and suppliers of the automotive industry. I think this is a great opportunity.”\n“The biggest advantage we have in Germany is our so-called Mittelstand,” Merz said. “Please open your doors for AI to make use of your data. This is a real secret and a real advantage for Germany to make use of all the data which is already available in our companies.”\nHuang agreed, and praised Germany’s world-class expertise in manufacturing companies both large and small, as well as its math and science excellence. That national expertise and knowledge is what sets the German economy apart, he said, and AI will only amplify it.\n“That knowledge belongs to you,” Huang said. “It cannot be replaced. AI can only augment your capability, it will not replace your capability. Take advantage of the technology. It’s a new instrument.”\nGermany’s expertise with physical technologies is a differentiator, Huang and Merz agreed, and a powerful accelerator for the German economy when AI is included.\n“If we are successful in bringing these two aspects and parts of the business of the future together,” Merz said. “I’m really very optimistic that Germany can be one of the most successful economies in the world.”\n“This industrial revolution is so mighty, so powerful,” Huang said. “In the Chancellor’s words, you are re-engineering Germany. This is your chance to do it well.”\n“I would like to see him regularly, let’s say at least once a year, and discuss the progress we made,” Merz said, “And see a guy sitting in my office being extremely happy about Germany as a place to invest.”\nHuang also announced new investments in four German startups, part of a nearly $10 billion commitment by NVIDIA to support companies, universities, research institutions and more across Europe.\nNVIDIA has been investing across Germany’s startup ecosystem, supporting leading technology companies including n8n, Neura Robotics and Black Forest Labs .\nAgile Robots, a Munich-based robotics company, builds AI-powered robots, from robotic arms to its Agile ONE humanoid, for manufacturing, logistics and healthcare.\nAvelios Medical, a Munich-based healthcare technology startup, develops an AI-native hospital information system that serves as the core system of record for clinical and administrative workflows, integrating AI directly into hospital operations to free up doctors and nurses to deliver better patient care.\nMirelo , a German startup, develops AI models that generate sound effects, speech and music for video, helping creators and production teams produce high-quality audio that perfectly matches their visuals on screen.\nReflex Aerospace, a Berlin-based space scaleup, designs and builds high-performance satellites ready for launch in about 12 months instead of years.\nEarlier in the day, Siemens announced it’s partnering with NVIDIA to advance AI-driven, autonomous factories. Siemens showcased new AI solutions at its Erlangen factory , advancing its role as a global blueprint for autonomous manufacturing. Fully virtual production ramp-up reduces engineering effort by up to 85%, while agent-based production planning enables more adaptive manufacturing and cost savings of up to 20%.\nNVIDIA researchers used JUPITER, Europe’s largest supercomputer at the Jülich Supercomputing Centre, to run a record-breaking simulation of a fault-tolerant quantum computer. The 104-qubit simulation is the largest and most detailed of its kind to date.\n\n### germany-startups gnews:34d7578bfc7ff1726474fe55 \n Nvidia's GTC Berlin Opens Tomorrow: Jensen Huang to Take the Stage With $10 Billion European AI Investment Plan\nNvidia's GTC conference will be held in Berlin, Germany, from October 20 to 22, with Jensen Huang delivering the keynote on the 21st. This year's event shifts focus from selling GPUs to promoting AI factories, featuring more than 100 sessions covering accelerated computing, agentic AI, physical AI, robotics and open-source models. Vera Rubin has entered full-scale production, with first shipments beginning this fall; AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure will be among the first cloud providers to deploy it in 2026. On the European front, Nvidia has invested about $8 billion over the past year and plans to add roughly $2 billion, bringing the total to nearly $10 billion. On October 19, Germany's federal government and Nvidia signed a joint declaration of intent, with about 12 companies including Siemens, Audi and Black Forest Labs participating; Nvidia also announced investments in four German startups.\nNvidia's GTC conference will be held in Berlin, Germany, from October 20 to 22 local time, with founder and CEO Jensen Huang scheduled to deliver the keynote on the 21st from 11 a.m. to 1 p.m. Central European Summer Time — 5 p.m. to 7 p.m. Taipei time and 6 p.m. to 8 p.m. Japan time. It is Nvidia's highest-profile technology and ecosystem event of the second half, and the signal it sends is already clear: the chip giant is shifting its narrative from \"selling GPUs\" to \"selling AI factories.\"\nAccording to the official agenda, this year's GTC features more than 100 sessions spanning the full AI technology stack, with key themes including accelerated computing, agentic AI, physical AI, robotics, open-source models and AI infrastructure. For investors watching Nvidia, the event is both a test of its technology roadmap and an important window into the pace at which its European strategy is being implemented.\nOn the hardware side, the actual deployment progress of the Vera Rubin platform is widely seen as a key focal point. At the San Jose GTC in March this year, Nvidia positioned Vera Rubin as a full-stack computing platform for agentic AI, spanning CPUs, GPUs, networking and storage. What the market expects to see in Berlin is no longer another roadmap, but whether major cloud providers are truly entering the large-scale deployment phase, and whether per-token inference costs can fall substantially. Those two metrics bear directly on whether the AI factory model can move from concept to a replicable commercial loop.\nNotably, Vera Rubin's deployment progress is not entirely unknown. Nvidia announced on May 31 that the platform had entered full-scale production, with first shipments beginning this fall. According to the company, Vera Rubin delivers 10 times the throughput of the previous-generation Grace Blackwell on agentic workloads, cuts the number of GPUs needed to train mixture-of-experts models to one quarter, and can lower per-token inference costs to as little as one tenth. AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure will all be among the first cloud providers to deploy Vera Rubin instances in 2026, with CoreWeave, Lambda, Nebius and Nscale also on the initial list. In other words, what the Berlin event will test is not whether cloud providers are adopting the platform, but the speed and scale of adoption.\nThe platform upgrade is equally critical to Taiwan's supply chain. Nvidia said Vera Rubin systems are being mass-produced by Taiwanese server makers and global supply chain partners; of hundreds of supply chain partners, 150 are in Taiwan alone, spanning more than 350 factories across 30 countries.\nCompared with hardware specifications, Nvidia's capital moves in Europe may be more worth tracking. Taken together with the company's recent actions, Europe is being positioned as a key market for its next phase of AI ecosystem expansion.\nOn October 13, Huang said at an event in Paris that Nvidia would announce about $2 billion in new European AI investments or deals within the coming week. Over the past year, the company has already invested about $8 billion in Europe. Combined, Nvidia's European investments and commitments will approach $10 billion.\nWhile in Paris, Huang announced that Nvidia was providing financing to four French startups: health-tech company Doctolib, robotics AI company Genesis AI, software company Pigment and AI materials company Entalpic. Later, at an event in London, he named British AI companies Boltz, Prima Mente, Hiverge, Riverlane and Foresight. Judging from the distribution of targets, Nvidia's European buildout is not purely financial investment but an effort to weave an ecosystem around physical AI, materials discovery and industrial software. French server maker Bull also plans to produce Vera Rubin Ultra NVL72 systems in Europe starting in 2028, with trays manufactured in Europe and integrated at its Angers plant — a sign that Nvidia's European buildout is extending from investment into local manufacturing.\nOn October 14, Nvidia also announced in London a five-year, $250 million computing donation to support agent safety and security research in the U.K., along with an investment plan for British startups covering healthcare, AI research, infrastructure and quantum computing. As of October 20, Nvidia had announced investments in four French and four German startups during the week, without disclosing individual amounts or saying whether they make up the entirety of the roughly $2 billion in new spending previously flagged.\nGermany is likely to be the main battleground for this round of expansion. On October 19, German Federal Chancellor Friedrich Merz signed a joint declaration of intent with Nvidia at the \"Invest in Germany\" summit in Berlin. Germany's Federal Ministry for Economic Affairs and Energy and the Federal Ministry of Research, Technology and Space took part in the signing, and the cooperation will be advanced through Germany's Industry 4.0 platform. In a subsequent conversation, Merz said Germany is committed to becoming one of the most advanced locations for AI applications globally, not just within the EU. According to a prior report by Germany's Handelsblatt, the cooperation is expected to receive several hundred million euros in government funding, with about 12 German companies participating, focused on robotics, industrial manufacturing and autonomous driving. As an early example of the cooperation, a fully virtualized production ramp-up demonstrated at Siemens' Erlangen plant can reduce engineering workload by up to 85%, while agent-based production planning can save up to 20% in costs.\nAccording to the report, the cooperation project is based on Nvidia's Nemotron family of language models and its Cosmos physical AI models, with two interlinked goals: developing \"physical AI\" for robots and autonomous vehicles, and training industrial-scenario AI models that are internationally competitive. Participating companies include Siemens, Audi and Black Forest Labs, while Deutsche Telekom's industrial AI cloud platform will provide training infrastructure.\nNvidia also announced the same day that it was investing in four German startups: Munich robotics company Agile Robots, Munich medical AI company Avelios Medical, German sound, speech and music generation company Mirelo, and Berlin satellite company Reflex Aerospace. Together with earlier investments in n8n, Neura Robotics and Black Forest Labs, Nvidia's German footprint now spans robotics, healthcare, content generation and aerospace.\nIf the cooperation proceeds as planned, its significance goes beyond a single order. For Nvidia, it means its models and computing platform are being embedded in Europe's manufacturing system in the form of a \"national-level industrial cooperation\" for the first time; for German industry, it means importing an external technology foundation in areas where its own software and AI capabilities are relatively weak.\nPart of roughly $2 billion new plan; individual amounts undisclosed\nJoint declaration of intent with Germany's federal government; investment in Agile Robots, Avelios Medical, Mirelo, Reflex Aerospace\nGovernment funding of several hundred million euros (media report); corporate investment undisclosed\nThe total above corresponds to Nvidia's stated \"about $8 billion over the past year plus about $2 billion in new plans over the coming week\"; individual investment amounts are mostly undisclosed.\nGermany is not Nvidia's first \"national-level industrial cooperation.\" On July 16 this year, Nvidia partnered with Japan's Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry and Noetra to build what it calls the \"world's first national-level physical AI infrastructure\" — an AI factory with about 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs, providing 140 megawatts of data center capacity to support METI's FRONTia program. Noetra was initiated by core companies including Sony, SoftBank, NEC and Honda, with 44 companies and institutions contributing capital; the factory is scheduled to break ground in April 2027 and begin operations in June 2028. The Japanese government's goal is to capture more than 30% of the global AI robotics market by 2040 — an opportunity it estimates at about $133 billion.\nAbout 27,500 Rubin GPUs, 13,750 Vera CPUs, 140 MW\nGroundbreaking April 2027, operations June 2028\nAbout 12, including Siemens, Audi, Black Forest Labs\n44 investors and participants, including Sony, SoftBank, NEC, Honda\nThe two countries' approaches differ: Japan is moving forward with a clear computing scale and operational timeline, while Germany is first using a government declaration of intent to tie the Industry 4.0 platform to Nvidia's models and computing power, with specific investment and computing scale not yet disclosed. For Nvidia, both models point to the same goal — turning \"selling GPUs\" into \"selling AI factories\" and building a new growth pole outside the United States and mainland China.\nFrom a broader perspective, Nvidia's decision to step up in Europe now is closely tied to changes in the local regulatory environment and industrial policy. The EU has in recent years pushed ahead with a digital sovereignty agenda, with rising demands for localized computing infrastructure, and governments are willing to trade subsidies for AI capabilities on the ground. By entering through government cooperation combined with startup financing, Nvidia can avoid the policy friction that comes with simply selling hardware while locking in ecosystem partners early.\nIn terms of market impact, this year's GTC could shape expectations along three threads. First is the pace of Vera Rubin deployment: if major cloud providers confirm large-scale adoption, it would reinforce confidence in the revenue ramp for Nvidia's next-generation platform. Second is per-token inference cost: a clear decline in this metric would mean improved economics for AI applications, benefiting the entire downstream software and services chain. Third is the implementation of European investments and government cooperation, which offers room to imagine Nvidia opening a third growth pole beyond North America and mainland China.\nOf course, there is still a distance between announcing investments and generating meaningful revenue contribution. The maturity of Europe's AI ecosystem, the procurement pace of local companies and the subsidy approval processes of individual countries could all affect how quickly projects move forward. How many verifiable details Huang provides in his keynote on the 21st will be the first yardstick for judging the substance of this European push.\n\n### germany-startups gnews:5a071761d10eb93eb2baf8f5 \n Nvidia named Agile Robots, Avelios Medical, Mirelo and Reflex Aerospace as investment targets, part of nearly $10 billion commitment for Europe's AI ecosystem.\nNvidia expands collaboration with the German government on industrial AI, while Siemens announced a wider partnership with the chipmaker to advance AI-driven autonomous factories.\nBERLIN, GERMANY - OCTOBER 19: German Chancellor Friedrich Merz, and NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang toast with drinks while on stage at the Invest In Germany Summit to announce a strategic cooperation between the German government, NVIDIA and a group of German companies in artificial intelligence capacity for Germany industry on October 19, 2026 in Berlin, Germany. The project includes a significant investment by Germany in powerful NVIDIA chips. (Photo by Nadja Wohlleben/Getty Images)\nNvidia is increasing its investments in Europe's artificial intelligence sector, unveiling plans to back four German startups as the country pushes to strengthen its position in industrial AI.\nThe investments are part of the U.S. chipmaker's nearly $10 billion commitment to invest in companies, universities and research institutions across Europe, as announced at the Invest in Germany Summit in Berlin on Monday.\nThe four startups are Agile Robots, Avelios Medical, Mirelo and Reflex Aerospace. However, Nvidia did not disclose the size of the planned investments.\nAgile Robots develops AI-powered robots for manufacturing, logistics and healthcare, while Avelios Medical builds AI-powered hospital management software. Mirelo develops AI models that generate audio for videos, while Reflex Aerospace designs and builds satellites.\nThe announcement comes as Germany seeks to accelerate AI adoption across its industrial sector, with Chancellor Friedrich Merz saying the country aims to become a leader in integrating AI into key sectors.\nMerz, who described the technology as a major opportunity for the country's economy, named manufacturing, machinery and the automotive industry for integrating AI. He also urged Germany's small- and medium-sized businesses to capitalize on the data they already hold.\n\"Please open your doors for AI to make use of your data,\" Merz said. \"This is a real secret and a real advantage for Germany to make use of all the data which is already available in our companies.\"\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang echoed Merz's remarks while highlighting Germany's expertise in manufacturing, mathematics and science, saying that AI would strengthen rather than replace the country's industrial capabilities.\n\"AI can only augment your capability, it will not replace your capability,\" Huang said, describing the technology as a new instrument for businesses.\nNvidia is also expanding its collaboration with the German federal government and its \"Industry 4.0 Platform ,\" bringing together representatives from government, industry, research and trade associations, to accelerate the adoption of industrial AI. On Monday, the government signed a joint declaration of intent with Nvidia to advance AI models for industrial applications.\nEarlier in the day, Siemens had also announced new AI solutions developed with Nvidia at its factory in Erlangen, Germany. Siemens said the technologies could reduce engineering work involved in preparing production by up to 85%, while AI-powered production planning could cut costs by as much as 20%.\nHuang had also visited France last week, where he announced investments in four French startups and met with French President Emmanuel Macron to discuss AI development. The Nvidia boss is scheduled to deliver a keynote address in Berlin on Wednesday.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story,x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story,items in [(\\\"firmus-investment\\\",c[\\\"firmus-investment\\\"][3:]),(\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\",c[\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\"][:1])] for x in items]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; c=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/unsigned-deals/candidates.json\\\")); db=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",story,x[\\\"input_id\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",db.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for story,items in [(\\\"firmus-investment\\\",c[\\\"firmus-investment\\\"][3:]),(\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\",c[\\\"meta-stock-interest\\\"][:1])] for x in items]' \"","aggregated_output":"\n### firmus-investment gnews:414503b5d8d69a581a4aae01 \n Firmus' September 8 agreement with OpenAI connects fresh computing demand to two distinct public-market exposures: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ), whose platform will power expansion, and Blackstone Inc. (NYSE: BX ), whose managed funds led a $10 billion debt financing facility for Firmus' Australian infrastructure program.\nOpenAI agreed to contract dedicated computing capacity from two Malaysian sites under a multiyear partnership. Firmus said its total contracted capacity across all customers now exceeds 900 megawatts. That number must not be mistaken for the size of OpenAI's individual commitment.\nThe company also distinguished two operational sites from five under development, targeting availability over the next 24 months. Customer commitments support the expansion, but most sites still have to reach service.\nHardware demand and investment exposure differ\nFirmus plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera Rubin across Asia-Pacific. The potential benefit for NVIDIA is straightforward: successful development can create demand for accelerated computing systems and the accompanying platform.\nYet the announcement provides no NVIDIA order value or revenue schedule. Power, cooling, construction and customer deployment must align before a regional plan becomes an earnings contribution. Investors should not turn a capacity headline into a precise chip-sales forecast.\nBlackstone's connection comes through financing. In February, Firmus announced a $10 billion debt facility led by funds managed by Blackstone Tactical Opportunities, Blackstone Credit & Insurance and affiliates, supported by Coatue. Its stated purpose was the next phase of Project Southgate in Australia.\nThat is neither a claim that Blackstone's public parent supplied $10 billion from its own balance sheet nor evidence that the facility specifically finances the new Malaysian sites. The geography and legal investment vehicles limit what shareholders can infer.\nFor Blackstone, managing infrastructure investment capital can support fees and performance-related economics. The Firmus disclosures do not quantify those benefits. Project delays or weaker customer economics could also impair investment outcomes and complicate the broader fundraising opportunity.\nInsider Monkey's tracked worksheet sample counted 285 NVIDIA holders in Q2 2026 versus 275 in Q1, while Blackstone holders declined to 76 from 84. Ken Fisher's firm held NVIDIA, and D. E. Shaw held Blackstone. These historical positions preceded the OpenAI announcement.\nAugust 14 short interest represented 1.23% of NVIDIA's float and 2.94% of Blackstone's, providing dated positioning context rather than a verdict on the partnership.\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) benefits if customers can deploy and pay for more of its computing platform. Blackstone Inc. (NYSE:BX) needs attractive investment outcomes from capital it manages. OpenAI's commitment supports Firmus' demand case, while delivery and financing discipline still determine how that demand reaches either public company's shareholders.\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of NVDA and BX as investments, we believe certain other AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\n\n### firmus-investment gnews:3b7aa1d60555ee2b82322ede \n Nvidia ( NVDA , Financials ),-backed data center operator Firmus Grid has started premarketing a potential A$7 billion listing in Australia, giving investors another look at the infrastructure spending building around the AI boom.\nFirmus began speaking with investors Thursday ahead of its planned listing on the Australian Securities Exchange, according to IFR. A term sheet sent to investors said the institutional bookbuild is expected to run from Oct. 6 to Oct. 7, with the prospectus due Oct. 8.\nRetail investors are expected to be able to bid for shares from Oct. 12 through Oct. 19.\nThe company, founded in 2019 by Oliver Curtis, Tim Rosenfield and Jonathan Levee, could raise as much as $5.5 billion in the offering. An additional $500 million could be raised through a greenshoe option if demand allows.\nFirmus operates data center infrastructure, putting the planned IPO squarely in the middle of the massive investment cycle surrounding artificial intelligence and high-performance computing.\nFor Nvidia, the connection is another example of how demand for AI computing is extending beyond chips into the power and infrastructure needed to run increasingly large data centers.\nThe next catalyst is the start of Firmus' institutional bookbuild on Oct. 6, when investor demand should give a clearer picture of how the market values the AI infrastructure operator.\n\n### firmus-investment gnews:e47314cc30e2f058c7565b3f \n Nvidia-backed Firmus closes IPO bookbuilding as investor support falter -Bloomberg\nInvesting.com -- Australia-based data-center company Firmus Grid has closed bookbuilding for its IPO after investor concerns grew that the offering could be pulled, Bloomberg reported on Thursday, putting a planned multibillion-dollar listing in doubt.\nThe company was seeking to raise $5.5 billion including a greenshoe option, with an offer price of A$11 a share implying a valuation of A$43.7 billion ($30.4 billion).\nBookbuilding closed as scheduled on Thursday morning, but there was no clear indication of the final price or structure, according to people familiar with the matter cited by Bloomberg.\nFirmus had brought forward the bookbuilding deadline to Thursday from Friday after initially seeing demand exceed the offer size, Bloomberg reported.\nThe report highlighted that some prospective investors became more cautious over the possibility that existing shareholders could sell stock soon after the listing, creating an overhang.\nNot finding the right stocks? Our AI's list of investor-grade stock picks for October just dropped »\nThe uncertainty also hit Maas Group Holdings, a Firmus backer.\nIts shares fell as much as 30% in Sydney, their biggest recorded decline, as speculation mounted over whether the IPO would proceed. Maas said it was aware of market commentary but did not know of any undisclosed information explaining the share-price move.\nThe potential listing had been on track to rank among Australia's largest-ever IPOs, comparable with Medibank's offering in 2014, which raised just under $5 billion.\nFirmus began as a Bitcoin mining operation in Australia in 2019 and has since benefited from surging demand for AI infrastructure across Asia, building a pipeline of data-center projects in Australia and Singapore.\nThe company has secured $2 billion in commitments from investors including Nvidia and Blackstone, while Jane Street is another existing shareholder. It also raised $505 million in an April funding round led by Coatue Management.\nProceeds from the IPO would have been used to buy GPUs for Firmus's first data center in Batam, Indonesia, being developed with DayOne Data Centers under an eight-year partnership with Nvidia.\nThe struggles come as AI-related companies have driven a wave of fundraising across global equity markets, while investor sentiment has recently become more cautious over the pace of borrowing and spending.\nMusk accuses 'certain oligarchs' of blocking Starlink launch in India\nChinese stocks lose steam as trade resumes after Golden Week break\n\n### meta-stock-interest gnews:fe5a897258f58328337b5c30 \n / Meta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta's $35 Billion Stock Sale Draws Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom\nMeta Platforms META launched about $35 billion of concurrent public offerings to help fund its AI build-out, made up of $22.5 billion of Class A common stock sold through forward sale agreements and about $12.5 billion of mandatory convertible preferred depositary shares. Meta shares rose 1.15% in premarket.\nNvidia NVDA , Advanced Micro Devices AMD and Broadcom AVGO have indicated non-binding interest in buying up to an aggregate $5.0 billion of the Class A stock on the same terms as other buyers. Meta, which expects $135 billion to $145 billion of capital expenditures in 2026, said it will fund its investments through a mix of operating cash flow, investment-grade debt, strategic partnerships and equity.\nMeta also plans a roughly $60 billion at-the-market program in its new Class C common stock, expected to start in the first quarter of 2027 once those shares begin trading. The preferred shares will convert automatically into common stock around August 15, 2029, and the forward sales are expected to settle by November 19, 2027. Morgan Stanley and Goldman Sachs are lead bookrunners.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
